Level 13

This level was about using an SSH private key to log in to the next Bandit account.

I copied the private key to my machine and then changed its permissions using "chmod". After that, I used SSH with the "-i" option to specify the private key.

Commands used

chmod [permission] sshkey.private

Then I connected using the private key:

ssh -i ./sshkey.private bandit14@localhost -p 2220

After logging in, I used:

cat /etc/bandit_pass/bandit14

What I learned

What an SSH private key can be used for.
How to specify an SSH identity/private key using "-i".
Linux file permissions using "chmod".
Why private key files may need their permissions changed before SSH will accept them.
How to connect to another account using SSH key-based authentication.
Reflection

This level helped me understand that SSH does not always require a password. Authentication can also be performed using a private key.

Level 14

