Level 20

This was one of the hardest levels for me.

Because I had learned about OpenSSL in an earlier level, I started thinking that OpenSSL was related to encrypted connections, while Netcat ("nc") was mainly for unencrypted connections. I also initially misunderstood other networking tools and what they were actually used for.

I read several manuals and tried Netcat in different ways, but I kept getting strange outputs and could not understand what was happening.

I tried to solve it myself, then searched on Google, and finally checked a YouTube tutorial when I still could not figure it out.

The tutorial showed me a method using "echo" and Netcat. One thing that confused me was the "&" at the end of the command.

I wanted to understand why it was there, so I initially left it out. My terminal then appeared to be stuck because the process was still running in the foreground.

I learned that "&" allows a command to run in the background, which leaves the terminal available for other commands.

Commands / Concepts I Used

A listener was started using Netcat with UDP:

echo "" | nc -nlup &

After starting the listener, I used the provided "suconnect" program to connect to it:

./suconnect

I had to use another port because the ports I was experimenting with were already in use.

What I Learned

Netcat ("nc") can be used for network communication.
TCP and UDP are different transport protocols.
"-l" puts Netcat into listening mode.
"-u" selects UDP.
"-n" prevents DNS resolution.
"-p" specifies the local port.
"&" runs a command in the background.
A process running in the foreground can keep the terminal occupied.
A listener acts as the server side, while the program connecting to it acts as the client side.
I need to distinguish between tools based on their actual purpose instead of assuming that every networking tool deals with encryption.
Reflection

My biggest mistake in this level was that I kept trying to become the server when I actually needed to understand the client/server relationship required by the challenge.

I spent a lot of time reading manuals and experimenting with Netcat. I eventually used Google and then a YouTube tutorial to understand the intended approach.

The most useful thing I learned was not just the final command, but why the "&" was needed and how the listener and connecting program were supposed to interact.

This level was frustrating, but it gave me a much better understanding of processes, ports, listeners, clients, servers, TCP/UDP, and background processes.

