Level 31
This level was another difficult Git level for me.
Before starting, I watched a video about Git because I wanted to understand the basic Git workflow. Because of that, I already had some understanding of "git push", but I still did not fully understand what the challenge wanted me to do.
I searched Google and learned that I needed to create a file called "key.txt" and put the required content inside it.
I created the file using:
nano key.txt
I entered the required text and saved the file.
The challenge required the file to be pushed to the "master" branch. I initially tried to inspect my changes with Git commands, including "git show", and also got confused about the branch I was working on.
Eventually, when I tried to add the file, Git did not want to add it because the repository's ".gitignore" contained a rule for ".txt" files.
I learned that ".gitignore" tells Git which files it should normally ignore.
To add the required file anyway, I used:
git add -f key.txt
The "-f" option forces Git to add a file even when it is ignored.
After that, I committed the change:
git commit -m "add key"
I also learned that the commit message describes the change being committed.
Finally, I pushed the commit to the required branch:
git push origin master
The remote repository processed my push and returned the password for the next level.
What I Learned
How to create and edit a file using "nano".
How Git's staging area works.
"git add" stages changes for a commit.
"git add -f" can force-add an ignored file.
"git commit -m" creates a commit with a message.
"origin" is the name of the remote repository.
"master" is the branch being pushed to.
"git push origin master" pushes the local commit to the remote "master" branch.
Git has a complete workflow: create/change → stage → commit → push.
Reflection
