LS
Because Linux commands are generally case-sensitive, normal commands were not working as expected.
I started experimenting with shell variables and different ways of trying to get lowercase commands to work.
I tried things such as:
$Y=echo "ls"
and experimented with variables such as "$S". I also tried "$1", "$2", "$3", and "$4" after reading that positional parameters can have special meanings in shell scripting.
None of these attempts worked.
I then searched for the solution and learned about "$0". After understanding what "$0" represents in the context of a shell/script, I was able to use it to get back to a normal shell environment.
From there, normal commands started working again.
I then used:
ls
After finding the required information, I moved to the parent directory:
cd ..
Then I entered the next level's directory/account context and read its README:
cd bandit33 cat README
This allowed me to complete the final Bandit level.
What I Learned
Shell commands are case-sensitive.
A shell can modify or interpret input before executing it.
Shell variables can have special meanings.
"$0" has a special meaning in shell scripting and can refer to the name/path used to invoke the shell or script.
Positional parameters such as "$1", "$2", etc. have special meanings in shell scripts.
Understanding how the shell interprets input can be just as important as knowing Linux commands.
Reflection
This level involved a lot of experimentation.
I initially tried to force lowercase commands using variables and experimented with "$1", "$2", "$3", and "$4", but I did not understand what I was doing.
Eventually, I checked the solution and learned about "$0". Once I understood why it worked, I was able to regain a normal shell and complete the level.
This level taught me that when a command is not behaving normally, I should investigate the shell itself rather than assuming that the command is broken.
